        key_points: ['Ordinary Bill may begin in either House', 'Both Houses must pass the same text', 'Article 108 joint sitting for specified deadlocks', 'No joint sitting for Money or Constitution Amendment Bills', 'President may return an ordinary Bill once.'],
        examples: ['Choosing whether a deadlock can go to joint sitting', 'Tracing an ordinary Bill from introduction to assent'],
        comparison_points: ['Ordinary Bills permit joint sitting in specified deadlocks; Constitution Amendment Bills require separate passage without joint sitting.', 'A Bill pending in Rajya Sabha has different lapse consequences from one pending in Lok Sabha after dissolution.'],
        common_misconceptions: ['Every disagreement between the Houses automatically leads to a joint sitting.', 'The President can return a Constitution Amendment Bill under Article 111.'],
        exam_focus_points: ['Revise Article 108 and its exclusions.', 'Remember lapse rules when Lok Sabha is dissolved.', 'Keep Article 111 ordinary-Bill action separate from Article 368.']
