        key_points: ['Cold weather: December-February', 'Hot weather: March-May', 'Southwest monsoon: June-September', 'Retreating monsoon: October-November', 'Regional variation is important.'],
        examples: ['Matching a weather phenomenon with season', 'Choosing the main rainfall season for a region'],
        comparison_points: ['Cold season is generally dry in north India but can have western-disturbance rain; retreating monsoon is dry for much of north India but rainy for Tamil Nadu.', 'Pre-monsoon showers precede the main monsoon; retreating rains follow southwest monsoon withdrawal.'],
        common_misconceptions: ['India has uniform seasons on the same dates everywhere.', 'All winter rain in India is caused by the northeast monsoon.'],
        exam_focus_points: ['Revise season-month-phenomenon pairs.', 'Link western disturbances with winter and Tamil Nadu with retreating monsoon.', 'Remember transition-weather terms.']
